  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA low pressure system will continue to move north up the East\nCoast today, keeping the entire area on the western edge of the\nsystem. As a result, the swaths of rain continue to rotate\nacross the area allowing for the dreary and rainy conditions to\nlinger. These conditions are expected to continue through\ntonight, gradually drying from west to east as the parent low\npushes further northeast towards Maine. Although the bulk of\nprecipitation is expected to remain as rain, there is a\npotential for a few flakes to mix in, especially across the\nsoutheastern counties and into NWPA where temperatures have\nremained in the 30s today and 850mb temperatures of -2 to -3C. \nNo snow accumulation is expected. Additional rainfall of less \nthan 0.3\" is expected. Overnight lows will fall into the low to\nmid 30s. In addition, breezy conditions are expected to linger \nthrough this evening with gusts of 20 to 30 mph, especially \nclosest to the lakeshore. A few lingering showers are possible \nearly Saturday morning before a weak ridge builds over the area \nand allows for a dry day with highs in the low 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nOn Sunday, a robust upper level trough and deepening surface low\nwill move east through the Great Lakes region resulting in\nanother period of widespread rainfall. With this system, a cold\nfront is expected to move east and provide an additional focus\nfor the precipitation and also a transition to a much cooler\nairmass. On Sunday, highs will climb into the mid to upper 40s\nbefore the front moves east. By Monday, highs will only rise\ninto the mid to upper 30s. This shift in temperatures profile\nshould initially allow all precipitation to remain as rain, but\nbehind the front cannot rule out a few flakes also occurring. As\nthe parent low shifts east on Monday, a surface trough will\nlinger a bit and likely result in additional lake effect showers\nwith a rain/snow mix possible. Once again, not expecting any\naccumulations. Outside of precipitation chances, will also \nhave to keep an eye on the wind forecast for Sunday night into \nMonday as robust CAA could assist in mixing the strong LLJ down \nto the surface. If that happens, stronger winds across the area \nwith gusts up to 45 mph will be possible.",
